Keep archive names as paths until they are encoded

AddFiles computes the name a file gets inside the archive by calling
u8string() on its relative path and handing the result to
WindowsifyFilePath(const fs::path &). The std::string does not match that
parameter, so it is quietly turned back into a path through the narrow-string
constructor -- and on Windows the C++ runtimes read a narrow string
differently: libstdc++ as UTF-8, libc++ (like MSVC's STL) in the ANSI code
page. Under libc++ the UTF-8 bytes of a non-ASCII name are therefore decoded
as, say, Windows-1251 and encoded again, and "日本語.txt" is stored in the
archive as "ж—Ґжњ¬иЄћ.txt".

Pass the path itself; WindowsifyFilePath already does the one u8string()
conversion. Nothing changes where a narrow string is UTF-8 anyway (libstdc++,
every non-Windows platform).

The bug used to stay hidden: with an ANSI StormLib, adding a file with a
non-ASCII name failed outright. Now that 0002 hands StormLib the native wide
path the add succeeds, so the name has to be right.

Upstream: not submitted yet
Applies-to: all environments (behaviour changes only on Windows with libc++ or MSVC)

--- a/src/mpq.cpp
+++ b/src/mpq.cpp
@@ -250,10 +250,10 @@
         std::string archive_file_path;
 
         if (path_prefix.empty()) {
-            archive_file_path = WindowsifyFilePath(input_file_path.u8string());
+            archive_file_path = WindowsifyFilePath(input_file_path);
         } else {
             archive_file_path =
-                WindowsifyFilePath((fs::path(path_prefix) / input_file_path).u8string());
+                WindowsifyFilePath(fs::path(path_prefix) / input_file_path);
         }
 
